{"book":"Genesis","chapter":5,"coverage":{"category_diversity":3,"entity_counts":{"artifacts":0,"events":0,"groups":0,"people":0,"places":0},"evidence_categories":{"culture":12,"geography":2,"history":5},"evidence_item_count":19,"relationship_unit_count":4,"specific_evidence_count":11,"unit_kind_counts":{"interpretive_questions":1,"language_literary":1,"surrounding_passages":5,"why_it_matters":2},"unused_evidence_ids":["image-of-god-theme:ancient_near_east_context:0","image-of-god-theme:hebraic_worldview:0","image-of-god-theme:historical_context:0","image-of-god-theme:second_temple_context:0","what-is-the-image-of-god:ancient_near_east_context:0","what-is-the-image-of-god:historical_context:0","why-are-genealogies-included:ancient_near_east_context:0","why-are-genealogies-included:historical_context:0"],"used_evidence_ids":["genesis-5-adam-seth-image-lineage","genesis-5-and-he-died-mortality","genesis-5-enoch-walks-with-god","genesis-5-genealogy-narrative-function","genesis-5-methuselah-lamech-noah-naming","genesis-5-repeated-genealogy-formula","genesis-ane-comparative-context","genesis-literary-movement","genesis:interpretive_note:0","genesis:interpretive_note:1","genesis:interpretive_note:2"]},"evidence_availability":"AVAILABLE","evidence_bundle_version":"1.0","evidence_gaps":[{"category":"language_literary","reason":"No language evidence was supplied.","severity":"informational"}],"evidence_hash":"a8b67cd1ddbf43d5c1b6086118046a6e6c15dc33a4e616fcab8233ceacd3db7e","reference":"Genesis 5","synthesis_compiler_version":"1.1","synthesis_hash":"444a3d600dba4414b10bf6fb728da75b983cb5b00227ae155ca9acc83a52b418","synthesis_schema_version":"1.1","synthesis_units":[{"confidence":"medium","entity_ids":[],"evidence_ids":["genesis-5-enoch-walks-with-god","genesis-5-genealogy-narrative-function","genesis-5-methuselah-lamech-noah-naming"],"facts":["Genesis 5:21-24 gives Enoch a distinct notice: he fathers Methuselah, walks with God for 300 years, and is said to have been taken by God rather than receiving the ordinary death formula.","As a literary unit, Genesis 5 preserves a continuous Adam-to-Noah line while carrying forward both human mortality and the hope attached to Noah; the chapter's function is therefore narrative continuity and thematic development, not an explicit modern chronology table.","Genesis 5:25-32 carries the line through Methuselah and Lamech to Noah and explains Noah's name through Lamech's hope for relief from the labor and pain associated with the ground that the LORD had cursed."],"id":"syn_interpretive_questions_867fda086147","interpretation_level":"disputed","kind":"interpretive_questions","metadata":{"explicit_significance":false,"passage_scope":"CURRENT_CHAPTER","relationship_basis":"shared_parent_object","source_categories":["culture","history"],"support_count":3},"passage_scope":"CURRENT_CHAPTER","related_unit_ids":[],"source_anchors":["Genesis 5:1-32","Genesis 5:21-24","Genesis 5:25-32"],"verse_refs":["Genesis 5:1-32","Genesis 5:21-24","Genesis 5:25-32"]},{"confidence":"high","entity_ids":[],"evidence_ids":["genesis-5-adam-seth-image-lineage","genesis-5-and-he-died-mortality","genesis-5-repeated-genealogy-formula"],"facts":["Genesis 5:1-3 introduces the written account of Adam's generations, recalls humanity made in God's likeness, and follows Adam's line through Seth, whom Adam fathers in his own likeness and image.","The repeated words 'and he died' in Genesis 5:5, 8, 11, 14, 17, 20, 27, and 31 create a mortality refrain across the genealogy, while the exception of Enoch in verses 21-24 interrupts the pattern.","Genesis 5 repeatedly uses a genealogy formula—age at fathering, additional years, total lifespan, and a closing notice—so the chapter's regular pattern organizes the succession from Adam toward Noah."],"id":"syn_language_literary_1af10b5a1a7c","interpretation_level":"fact","kind":"language_literary","metadata":{"explicit_significance":false,"passage_scope":"CURRENT_CHAPTER","relationship_basis":"shared_parent_object","source_categories":["culture"],"support_count":3},"passage_scope":"CURRENT_CHAPTER","related_unit_ids":[],"source_anchors":["Genesis 5:1-3","Genesis 5:25-32","Genesis 5:3-20","Genesis 5:5-31"],"verse_refs":["Genesis 5:1-3","Genesis 5:25-32","Genesis 5:3-20","Genesis 5:5-31"]},{"confidence":"medium","entity_ids":[],"evidence_ids":["genesis:interpretive_note:1","genesis:interpretive_note:2"],"facts":["Read Genesis 1-11 as ancient theological and literary discourse before imposing modern scientific genres or dismissing its claims as merely decorative myth.","Ancient Near Eastern parallels can clarify shared motifs and cultural questions, but parallels alone do not prove direct copying or settle the interpretation of Genesis."],"id":"syn_surrounding_passages_1d775faf9923","interpretation_level":"disputed","kind":"surrounding_passages","metadata":{"explicit_significance":false,"relationship_basis":"shared_parent_object","safe_for_significance":true,"support_count":2},"passage_scope":"SURROUNDING_PASSAGE","related_unit_ids":["syn_surrounding_passages_41f53aa38451"],"source_anchors":["Genesis 1:1-11:26"],"verse_refs":["Genesis 10:1-32","Genesis 11:1-26","Genesis 1:1-31","Genesis 2:1-25","Genesis 3:1-24","Genesis 4:1-26","Genesis 6:1-22","Genesis 7:1-24","Genesis 8:1-22","Genesis 9:1-29"]},{"confidence":"medium","entity_ids":[],"evidence_ids":["genesis:interpretive_note:1","genesis:interpretive_note:2"],"facts":["Read Genesis 1-11 as ancient theological and literary discourse before imposing modern scientific genres or dismissing its claims as merely decorative myth.","Ancient Near Eastern parallels can clarify shared motifs and cultural questions, but parallels alone do not prove direct copying or settle the interpretation of Genesis."],"id":"syn_surrounding_passages_41f53aa38451","interpretation_level":"disputed","kind":"surrounding_passages","metadata":{"explicit_significance":false,"passage_scope":"SURROUNDING_PASSAGE","relationship_basis":"shared_pa